 "audit": "Cohort is heterogeneous and noisy: ~100 diagnosis labels, many vague or uninformative ('Glioma, malignant', 'Neoplasm, malignant', 'missing', 'Unknown', 'Rhabdomyosarcoma, NOS'), so pooled labels mix biologically distinct subtypes and associations may reflect subtype composition. Mutation burden varies enormously (median 6, max 9075; a handful of hypermutated cases) and differs by diagnosis, so burden confounds gene-level tests; I excluded cases with >100 mutated genes and stratified by burden. Most diagnoses have very small n, so I only tested diagnoses with n>=8 and genes mutated in >=3 cases. About 245 tests were run (FDR corrected), and frequencies are likely inflated by selection. Possible batch/pipeline or sequencing-platform effects are not recorded. Large genes (e.g. mucins, AHNAK, DNAH) can show chance or artefactual enrichment and were not reported. Novel-looking hits (PIK3R1 in pilocytic astrocytoma, ZMYM3 in malignant glioma, ARID1B in RMS NOS) are statistically strong but need replication and variant-level review (variants look scattered rather than hotspot-like). I found far fewer than ten genuinely novel associations and did not pad the list.",
 "methods": "Excluded cases with >100 mutated genes. For each diagnosis (n>=8) and gene mutated in >=3 cases, ran a one-sided Cochran-Mantel-Haenszel test comparing that diagnosis with all other tumors, stratified by log2 mutation-burden bins. Applied Benjamini-Hochberg across ~245 tests and kept hits with q<1e-3 plus a few plausible established drivers with weaker support. Expected frequencies are observed fractions shrunk slightly; probabilities reflect significance and prior literature."
}